ML_PAGE("l5-subjective", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l5\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">CHAPTER 5 · SUBJECTIVE EXAM PREPARATION</span><h2>Writing Answers That Earn Marks</h2><p>Define, explain the mechanism, apply it to the scenario, then evaluate and conclude. Each mock question below has its full model answer behind a reveal; the complete subjective notes are underneath.</p></header><div class=\"theme-l5\"><div class=\"card accent-card mb-20\">\n    <h3>The four-move answer (10-20 mark questions)</h3>\n    <div class=\"flow-grid\">\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>1. Establish the principle</b><p>Define the concept accurately in the chapter's terms: portfolio return and σ, correlation, systematic vs unsystematic risk, beta, CAPM, SML, efficient frontier.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>2. Explain the mechanism</b><p>Show <em>why</em> the relationship exists: the economic cause, not just the result.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>3. Apply and analyse</b><p>Connect it to the scenario: compute E(R<sub>p</sub>), σ<sub>p</sub>, β<sub>p</sub> or the CAPM return, then interpret what it means for the whole portfolio.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>4. Evaluate and conclude</b><p>Weigh alternatives, name a limitation or assumption, and give a justified, conditional conclusion.</p></div>\n    </div>\n    <p class=\"card-note\">Chapter 5 answers must move from the single asset to the portfolio: what matters is how an investment changes the risk and return of everything the investor already holds.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"compare-box span-all\">\n    <div class=\"compare-side\">\n        <h4>Weak answer</h4>\n        <p><b>Q:</b> Evaluate whether including a high-risk stock necessarily increases portfolio risk.</p>\n        <p>\"Including a high-risk stock increases portfolio risk because its standard deviation is high. Investors should avoid risky stocks if they want a safe portfolio.\"</p>\n        <p class=\"note-weak\">Confuses individual risk with the stock's contribution to portfolio risk.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"compare-side highlight\">\n        <h4>Strong answer</h4>\n        <p>\"A stock's standard deviation measures the dispersion of its own returns, but its effect on portfolio risk also depends on its weight and its correlation with the existing holdings. Assets with less than perfectly positive correlation partly offset each other, so adding a volatile stock can lower total portfolio σ if the diversification benefit is large enough: in the chapter's IBM-Celgene example the combined portfolio is less volatile than IBM alone. Correlations and weights can change, though. The stock should be judged by its contribution to portfolio risk, not rejected for its standalone volatility.\"</p>\n        <p class=\"note-good\">Mechanism + textbook evidence + conditions + justified conclusion.</p>\n    </div>\n</div>\n</div><h3 class=\"block-title\">Mock examination: questions with model answers</h3><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><span>Portfolio objectives, return and standard deviation (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>An investor wishes to construct a portfolio using the following securities:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Security</th>\n<th>Portfolio weight</th>\n<th>Expected return</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Equity A</td>\n<td>50%</td>\n<td>12%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Equity B</td>\n<td>30%</td>\n<td>8%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bonds C</td>\n<td>20%</td>\n<td>5%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Separately, the investor is evaluating a two-asset portfolio with 50% invested in Security X and 50% in Security Y.</p>\n<p>Security X has a standard deviation of 20%, Security Y has a standard deviation of 10%, and their correlation coefficient is +0.50.</p>\n<p>(a) [4 marks] Explain the objectives of growth-oriented and income-oriented portfolios and define an efficient portfolio.</p>\n<p>(b) [4 marks] Calculate the expected return of the three-security portfolio.</p>\n<p>(c) [8 marks] Calculate the standard deviation of the equally weighted two-security portfolio. Recalculate it assuming correlation falls to -0.50.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Evaluate why portfolio risk cannot generally be determined by taking the weighted average of individual securities' standard deviations.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Understand → Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 1 · High priority</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Portfolio objectives and efficiency</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>A portfolio is a collection of investments assembled to achieve one or more investment goals.</p>\n<p>The chapter distinguishes two major portfolio orientations.</p>\n<p>A growth-oriented portfolio emphasizes long-term capital appreciation. It may include securities whose main expected benefit is an increase in market value rather than regular current income.</p>\n<p>An income-oriented portfolio emphasizes cash distributions, including dividends and interest payments. It may be appropriate for investors requiring regular investment income.</p>\n<p>The ultimate objective is to construct an efficient portfolio.</p>\n<p>The chapter defines an efficient portfolio as one providing the highest expected return for a given level of risk.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Efficiency}=\\text{Best Attainable Risk-Return Trade-off}}$$</div>\n<p>An efficient portfolio is not necessarily the one with the highest absolute expected return. A higher-return portfolio may also involve substantially more risk.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Expected portfolio return</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter states that portfolio return is the weighted average of the constituent securities' expected returns.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{E(R_p)=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}w_iE(R_i)}$$</div>\n<p>Substituting:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R_p)=0.50(12\\%)+0.30(8\\%)+0.20(5\\%)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=6\\%+2.4\\%+1\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{E(R_p)=9.4\\%}$$</div>\n<p>The portfolio is expected to produce a 9.4% return based on the stated estimates.</p>\n<p>Its actual return may differ because expected returns are not guaranteed.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Calculating two-asset portfolio standard deviation</strong> [8 marks]</p>\n<p>Unlike expected return, portfolio standard deviation depends on correlations between asset returns.</p>\n<p>The two-asset portfolio variance formula is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ \\sigma_p^2= w_X^2\\sigma_X^2+ w_Y^2\\sigma_Y^2+ 2w_Xw_Y\\sigma_X\\sigma_Y\\rho_{XY} }$$</div>\n<p>The portfolio standard deviation is the square root of that variance.</p>\n<p>Given:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$w_X=w_Y=0.50$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sigma_X=0.20,\\qquad\\sigma_Y=0.10$$</div>\n<p>Situation 1: Correlation = +0.50</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned} \\sigma_p^2={}&amp;(0.5)^2(0.2)^2\\\\ &amp;+(0.5)^2(0.1)^2\\\\ &amp;+2(0.5)(0.5)(0.2)(0.1)(0.5) \\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=0.0100+0.0025+0.0050$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=0.0175$$</div>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sigma_p=\\sqrt{0.0175}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\sigma_p=13.23\\%}$$</div>\n<p>Situation 2: Correlation = -0.50</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned} \\sigma_p^2={}&amp;(0.5)^2(0.2)^2\\\\ &amp;+(0.5)^2(0.1)^2\\\\ &amp;+2(0.5)(0.5)(0.2)(0.1)(-0.5) \\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=0.0100+0.0025-0.0050$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=0.0075$$</div>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sigma_p=\\sqrt{0.0075}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\sigma_p=8.66\\%}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Effect of correlation on portfolio volatility</strong> (equal weights, individual volatilities of 20% and 10%):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\"> correlation ρ    portfolio σ\n     +0.5   ██████████████████████████          13.23%\n     -0.5   █████████████████                    8.66%\n            0%      4%      8%      12%     16%\n</code></pre>\n<p>Only the correlation changes. Portfolio weights and individual standard deviations remain constant.</p>\n<p>The portfolio's standard deviation declines from 13.23% to 8.66%.</p>\n<p>This reduction occurs because negative correlation allows movements in one investment to partially offset movements in the other.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Why portfolio risk is not a weighted average</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>A simple weighted average of individual standard deviations would produce:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$0.5(20\\%)+0.5(10\\%)=15\\%$$</div>\n<p>However, that result would apply to this long-only two-asset portfolio if the securities were perfectly positively correlated.</p>\n<p>The calculated portfolio standard deviations of 13.23% and 8.66% are lower because the correlation is less than +1.</p>\n<p>This demonstrates that the risk of combining investments depends not only on their individual volatility, but also on how their returns move together.</p>\n<p>The chapter's IBM-Celgene example provides similar evidence. It reports individual standard deviations of approximately 28.6% for IBM and 58.3% for Celgene, yet a portfolio weighted approximately 86% IBM and 14% Celgene has a standard deviation of approximately 25.3%.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: An investor should not reject a security solely because its individual volatility is high. The relevant question is how including that security affects the risk and expected return of the entire portfolio.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><span>Correlation, diversification and international investing (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>An analyst examines three hypothetical securities, X, Y and Z, using the return patterns from the chapter's Table 5.3.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>X</th>\n<th>Y</th>\n<th>Z</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>8%</td>\n<td>24%</td>\n<td>8%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n<td>20%</td>\n<td>12%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>12%</td>\n<td>16%</td>\n<td>16%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>14%</td>\n<td>12%</td>\n<td>20%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>16%</td>\n<td>8%</td>\n<td>24%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Two portfolios are constructed:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$XY=\\frac23X+\\frac13Y$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$XZ=\\frac23X+\\frac13Z$$</div>\n<p>(a) [6 marks] Calculate the annual returns and average returns of both portfolios.</p>\n<p>(b) [4 marks] Compare their standard deviations and explain why their risk differs despite having similar average returns.</p>\n<p>(c) [4 marks] Explain how international diversification may improve portfolio performance, using the chapter's discussion of economic cycles and investment opportunities.</p>\n<p>(d) [6 marks] Critically evaluate the statement that perfectly negative correlation always eliminates investment risk, and explain the limitations of international diversification.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 2 · High priority</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Portfolio XY and XZ expected returns</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter's Table 5.3 uses the following asset combinations:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{XY}=\\frac23R_X+\\frac13R_Y$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{XZ}=\\frac23R_X+\\frac13R_Z$$</div>\n<p>For Year 1:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{XY}=\\frac23(8\\%)+\\frac13(24\\%)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=13.33\\%$$</div>\n<p>For the XZ portfolio:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{XZ}=\\frac23(8\\%)+\\frac13(8\\%)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=8\\%$$</div>\n<p>Repeating these calculations:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>XY return</th>\n<th>XZ return</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>13.33%</td>\n<td>8.00%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>13.33%</td>\n<td>10.67%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>13.33%</td>\n<td>13.33%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>13.33%</td>\n<td>16.00%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>13.33%</td>\n<td>18.67%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Average</td>\n<td>13.33%</td>\n<td>13.33%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Both portfolios have approximately equal average returns.</p>\n<p>However, their return patterns are substantially different.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Comparing risk</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>Portfolio XY produces approximately 13.33% every year in the table.</p>\n<p>Therefore, its standard deviation across the five illustrated returns is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\sigma_{XY}=0\\%}$$</div>\n<p>Portfolio XZ produces fluctuating returns.</p>\n<p>Using the sample standard deviation formula:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$s_{XZ}= \\sqrt{ \\frac{\\sum(R_{XZ,t}-\\bar R_{XZ})^2}{5-1} }$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{s_{XZ}\\approx4.22\\%}$$</div>\n<p>The chapter reports approximately 4.2% for portfolio XZ and 0% for portfolio XY.</p>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>X and Y move in opposite directions. As X's return increases, Y's return decreases.</p>\n<p>The selected portfolio weights allow their movements to cancel.</p>\n<p>By contrast, X and Z both increase through the five illustrated years, so their fluctuations reinforce one another instead of canceling.</p>\n<p>Therefore, XY dominates XZ under the chapter's measured return and risk dimensions.</p>\n<p>The important result is not that X or Y individually became safer. Their combination produced a more stable portfolio.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Advantages of international diversification</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter identifies international diversification as a way of expanding investment opportunities beyond the domestic securities market.</p>\n<p>First, foreign markets provide access to a broader range of securities and industries.</p>\n<p>Second, different economies may experience different business cycles. One market might be contracting while another is expanding.</p>\n<p>If the returns from these markets are not perfectly positively correlated, combining them may reduce overall portfolio volatility.</p>\n<p>Third, the slides suggest that research opportunities may exist in foreign markets that are less informationally efficient, although this is a potential opportunity rather than a guaranteed advantage.</p>\n<p>Thus, international diversification can potentially improve the portfolio's risk-return trade-off by introducing investments with different economic exposures.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Evaluating perfect negative correlation and international risks</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter explains that perfectly negative correlation can eliminate portfolio risk.</p>\n<p>However, this conclusion requires clarification.</p>\n<p>A correlation of -1 means two return series have an exact negative linear relationship. It does not mean any arbitrary combination of those assets must have zero standard deviation.</p>\n<p>For two assets to achieve complete variance cancellation, their portfolio weights must be appropriate.</p>\n<p>The relevant condition is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{w_X\\sigma_X=w_Y\\sigma_Y}$$</div>\n<p>when:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\rho_{XY}=-1$$</div>\n<p>For example, if one asset has a standard deviation of 20% and another has 10%, equal weighting does not fully offset their volatility.</p>\n<p>A one-third allocation to the first asset and two-thirds to the second can achieve full cancellation under the idealized assumptions.</p>\n<p>That mathematical qualification goes beyond the simplified wording of the slide but follows directly from the portfolio-variance relationship.</p>\n<p>Real investment markets present further limitations.</p>\n<p>Correlations are estimated from data and may change over time. Assets that were historically negatively correlated may move in the same direction during an economic crisis.</p>\n<p>International diversification also introduces currency exchange risk, potentially higher investment costs and practical difficulties associated with foreign investing.</p>\n<p>The chapter identifies these disadvantages alongside the benefits of international diversification.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Lower correlation can reduce portfolio risk, but the extent of risk reduction depends on the actual return relationships and allocation weights. International diversification is a useful risk-management strategy, not a guarantee against investment losses.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><span>Systematic risk, unsystematic risk and beta (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A portfolio manager holds shares in 40 companies across different industries.</p>\n<p>One company experiences a major employee strike, while another suffers a significant legal dispute.</p>\n<p>At the same time, an economy-wide financial crisis causes most stock prices to decline.</p>\n<p>Two securities within the portfolio have the following characteristics:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Security A</th>\n<th>Security B</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Beta</td>\n<td>1.50</td>\n<td>0.60</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total standard deviation</td>\n<td>30%</td>\n<td>40%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>(a) [6 marks] Differentiate systematic and unsystematic risk, applying each to the scenario.</p>\n<p>(b) [6 marks] Explain the meaning of beta and calculate the market-related return movements for Securities A and B if the market declines by 8%.</p>\n<p>(c) [4 marks] Evaluate which security has greater market sensitivity and which has greater total volatility.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Explain why diversification cannot eliminate all portfolio risk and why CAPM emphasizes systematic risk.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Understand → Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 3 · High priority</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Components of portfolio risk</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter divides total portfolio risk into two broad components.</p>\n<p>Unsystematic risk, also called diversifiable risk, arises from firm-specific events.</p>\n<p>Examples include employee strikes, lawsuits and other developments affecting particular businesses.</p>\n<p>In this scenario, the employee strike and legal dispute represent unsystematic risks because their immediate causes are associated with individual companies.</p>\n<p>These risks may be reduced by holding a diversified portfolio rather than concentrating investment capital in only one or two firms.</p>\n<p>Systematic risk, also called nondiversifiable risk, arises from broader forces affecting many investments.</p>\n<p>Examples given in the chapter include war, inflation and political events.</p>\n<p>The economy-wide financial crisis represents systematic risk because it affects many securities simultaneously.</p>\n<p>The textbook's conceptual relationship is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Total Risk}=\\text{Systematic Risk}+\\text{Unsystematic Risk}}$$</div>\n<p>Diversification may reduce the unsystematic component substantially, but it does not eliminate the broad market exposure shared by many securities.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Beta and market-related returns</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter defines beta as a measure of nondiversifiable risk, showing how a security responds to market forces.</p>\n<p>The overall market has:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\beta_m=1}$$</div>\n<p>A security with beta greater than 1 is more sensitive to market movements than the benchmark.</p>\n<p>A security with beta between 0 and 1 has lower positive market sensitivity.</p>\n<p>The chapter illustrates the approximate market-related movement using:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Market-linked Return Movement}\\approx\\beta_i\\times\\text{Market Movement}$$</div>\n<p>In this scenario, the market declines by 8%.</p>\n<p><strong>Security A</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\beta_A=1.50$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$1.50(-8\\%)=-12\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Estimated market-linked movement}=-12\\%}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Security B</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\beta_B=0.60$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$0.60(-8\\%)=-4.8\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Estimated market-linked movement}=-4.8\\%}$$</div>\n<p>Security A has greater sensitivity to market fluctuations.</p>\n<p>However, these are simplified market-related estimates, consistent with the slide's interpretation of beta. They do not guarantee either security's actual return, which can also be influenced by firm-specific factors.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Beta versus standard deviation</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The securities have the following characteristics:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Measure</th>\n<th>Security A</th>\n<th>Security B</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Beta</td>\n<td>1.50</td>\n<td>0.60</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Standard deviation</td>\n<td>30%</td>\n<td>40%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Security A has the higher beta, so it is more sensitive to market movements.</p>\n<p>Security B has the higher standard deviation, so its total returns are more volatile based on the given measure.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Higher Beta}=\\text{Greater Market Sensitivity}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Higher SD}=\\text{Greater Total Return Volatility}}$$</div>\n<p>It would be incorrect to declare that Security A is more volatile in every sense merely because its beta is larger.</p>\n<p>Likewise, Security B should not automatically be considered more exposed to systematic market movements just because its standard deviation is higher.</p>\n<p>This distinction is important because beta and standard deviation measure different dimensions of investment risk.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Why systematic risk matters for CAPM</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>As more appropriately diversified securities are added to a portfolio, firm-specific fluctuations can increasingly offset one another.</p>\n<p>However, common market forces may affect many securities simultaneously.</p>\n<p>The chapter's Figure 5.8 illustrates portfolio risk declining as diversification increases before approaching a residual systematic-risk level.</p>\n<p>CAPM focuses on systematic risk because the model assumes investors can diversify away firm-specific risk.</p>\n<p>Consequently, under its framework, investors are compensated for bearing nondiversifiable market exposure rather than every source of individual asset volatility.</p>\n<p>This explains why a security with a high standard deviation does not automatically require a higher CAPM return than a security with lower standard deviation.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Beta helps measure market-related risk, while standard deviation measures total volatility. A well-diversified investor must distinguish between these risk measures rather than treating all investment uncertainty as equivalent.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><span>CAPM and the Security Market Line (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>An investment analyst assumes:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_f=6\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R_m)=10\\%$$</div>\n<p>The analyst evaluates three securities:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Security A</th>\n<th>Security B</th>\n<th>Security C</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Beta</td>\n<td>0.50</td>\n<td>1.00</td>\n<td>1.50</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Analyst's expected return</td>\n<td>9%</td>\n<td>9%</td>\n<td>14%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>(a) [4 marks] Explain the economic purpose of CAPM and derive its relationship between the risk-free rate, market risk premium and beta.</p>\n<p>(b) [6 marks] Calculate the CAPM-required return for each security.</p>\n<p>(c) [6 marks] Determine whether each security lies above or below the Security Market Line. Evaluate its attractiveness relative to the model.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Explain the intercept and slope of the SML and how changes in the risk-free rate or expected market risk premium affect the line.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 4 · Highest priority</em></p>\n<p>This question is particularly important because CAPM combines the chapter's treatment of systematic risk with the idea of a required investment return.</p>\n<p><strong>(a) CAPM and its economic meaning</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM) links an investment's required return to its exposure to systematic market risk.</p>\n<p>The model uses three inputs: the risk-free rate, the expected market return and the security's beta.</p>\n<p>The equation is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ R_i=R_f+\\beta_i[E(R_m)-R_f] }$$</div>\n<p>where:</p>\n<p>$R_i$ = CAPM-required return.</p>\n<p>$R_f$ = risk-free rate.</p>\n<p>$\\beta_i$ = security's systematic market-risk sensitivity.</p>\n<p>$E(R_m)$ = expected market return.</p>\n<p>The difference:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R_m)-R_f$$</div>\n<p>is the market risk premium.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ \\text{Required Return} = \\text{Risk-Free Rate} + \\text{Beta-Adjusted Market Risk Premium} }$$</div>\n<p>The risk-free rate establishes the baseline required return.</p>\n<p>The market risk premium compensates investors for bearing market-related risk, while beta determines how much of that compensation is required for a particular security.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Calculating CAPM-required returns</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>Given:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_f=6\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R_m)=10\\%$$</div>\n<p>The market risk premium is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$10\\%-6\\%=\\boxed{4\\%}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Security A</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_A=6\\%+0.5(4\\%)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_A=8\\%}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Security B</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_B=6\\%+1.0(4\\%)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_B=10\\%}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Security C</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_C=6\\%+1.5(4\\%)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_C=12\\%}$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Security</th>\n<th>Beta</th>\n<th>CAPM-required return</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>0.50</td>\n<td>8%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>1.00</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>1.50</td>\n<td>12%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>As beta increases, the required return increases because investors require more compensation for systematic market exposure.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Evaluating securities relative to the SML</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The Security Market Line (SML) represents the CAPM relationship graphically.</p>\n<p>The vertical axis shows required return, while the horizontal axis shows beta.</p>\n<p>For this example:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_i=6\\%+4\\%\\beta_i}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Security Market Line</strong> (CAPM with a 6% risk-free rate and a 4% market risk premium):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\"> required\n return\n  14% ┤                         ·\n      │                      ·\n  12% ┤                   ● C: β = 1.5 → 12%\n      │                ·\n  10% ┤             ● B: β = 1.0 → 10%\n      │          ·\n   8% ┤       ● A: β = 0.5 → 8%\n      │    ·\n   6% ┤ ● Rf (β = 0)\n      └────────────────────────────► beta (β)\n             0.5    1    1.5    2\n\n  A security ABOVE the line offers more than CAPM requires (positive alpha);\n  a security BELOW the line offers less (negative alpha).\n</code></pre>\n<p>Compare the required returns with the analyst's expected returns.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Security</th>\n<th>Expected return</th>\n<th>Required return</th>\n<th>Expected alpha</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>9%</td>\n<td>8%</td>\n<td>+1 percentage point</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>9%</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n<td>-1 percentage point</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>14%</td>\n<td>12%</td>\n<td>+2 percentage points</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Security A</strong></p>\n<p>The estimated expected return exceeds the CAPM-required return by one percentage point.</p>\n<p>The security therefore lies above the SML.</p>\n<p>Under the model and forecast assumptions, it provides more expected return than required for its beta.</p>\n<p><strong>Security B</strong></p>\n<p>The expected return is 9%, compared with a required return of 10%.</p>\n<p>Security B lies below the SML.</p>\n<p>The analyst's forecast does not provide sufficient expected compensation for its systematic risk under CAPM.</p>\n<p><strong>Security C</strong></p>\n<p>The expected return is 14%, compared with a required return of 12%.</p>\n<p>Security C lies above the SML by two percentage points.</p>\n<p>It has the largest positive expected alpha among the three securities.</p>\n<p>However, it also has the highest beta.</p>\n<p>Therefore, investors should not confuse the highest expected alpha with the lowest risk.</p>\n<p>Evaluation: Securities A and C appear attractive relative to CAPM, while Security B appears unattractive. But these conclusions depend on the accuracy of beta, expected returns and the model's assumptions.</p>\n<p>Positive expected alpha is not a guaranteed realized return.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Intercept and slope of the SML</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The equation is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_i=R_f+\\beta_i[E(R_m)-R_f]$$</div>\n<p>The vertical intercept is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_f=6\\%}$$</div>\n<p>This is the required return when beta equals zero.</p>\n<p>The slope is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{E(R_m)-R_f=4\\%}$$</div>\n<p>It represents the increase in required return for each additional unit of beta.</p>\n<p>If the risk-free rate increases while the market risk premium is held constant, the SML shifts upward in parallel.</p>\n<p>If the market risk premium increases while the risk-free rate is held constant, the SML becomes steeper.</p>\n<p>These effects differ because the intercept and slope represent different economic components.</p>\n<p>The chapter's Figure 5.6 uses a 6% risk-free rate and shows required returns increasing along the SML as beta increases.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: CAPM provides a benchmark for evaluating expected compensation for systematic risk. Investors should use it to compare expected returns with required returns rather than merely selecting securities with the highest beta or highest forecast return.</p>\n<p><strong>Examiner's key distinction:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Expected Return}&gt;\\text{CAPM Return}}$$</div>\n<p>This suggests a security lies above the SML under the model's assumptions.</p>\n<p>It does not prove that the investment will outperform in reality.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><span>Traditional portfolio management, MPT and the efficient frontier (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A financial advisor manages a client's wealth using investments in several established companies from different industries.</p>\n<p>The advisor believes the portfolio is well diversified simply because it contains 30 securities.</p>\n<p>A second advisor uses Modern Portfolio Theory to estimate expected returns, standard deviations and correlations between securities.</p>\n<p>The advisors are considering these hypothetical portfolios:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Portfolio</th>\n<th>Expected return</th>\n<th>Standard deviation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>P</td>\n<td>8%</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Q</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>R</td>\n<td>12%</td>\n<td>18%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>S</td>\n<td>11%</td>\n<td>16%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>(a) [6 marks] Compare the traditional portfolio approach with Modern Portfolio Theory.</p>\n<p>(b) [4 marks] Identify any dominated portfolio among the alternatives and justify your answer.</p>\n<p>(c) [6 marks] Explain the efficient frontier and how an investor's risk tolerance influences portfolio selection.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Recommend how the advisors should combine traditional and modern portfolio management principles.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Analyse → Evaluate → Create</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 5 · High priority</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Traditional versus modern portfolio management</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter distinguishes two main approaches to constructing investment portfolios.</p>\n<p>The traditional approach emphasizes balancing investments across a variety of securities and industries.</p>\n<p>It also tends to favor established, recognizable companies because investors may perceive them as safer, more liquid and easier to understand.</p>\n<p>In contrast, Modern Portfolio Theory (MPT) emphasizes statistical relationships among securities.</p>\n<p>It focuses on expected returns, standard deviations and correlations.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Dimension</th>\n<th>Traditional approach</th>\n<th>Modern Portfolio Theory</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Primary method</td>\n<td>Diversification across investments and industries</td>\n<td>Quantitative portfolio analysis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main consideration</td>\n<td>Investment characteristics and perceived suitability</td>\n<td>Expected returns, volatility and correlation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Typical emphasis</td>\n<td>Established securities and broad diversification</td>\n<td>Efficient risk-return combinations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main limitation</td>\n<td>Number and variety of holdings may not reveal their actual co-movement</td>\n<td>Results depend on the reliability of return and correlation estimates</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The central difference is that traditional management emphasizes diversification by security and industry, whereas MPT evaluates diversification through the relationships between securities' returns.</p>\n<p>A portfolio containing 30 stocks may appear diversified.</p>\n<p>However, if most securities respond similarly to market conditions, they may provide limited diversification benefits.</p>\n<p>Thus, the number of securities held is not enough to establish portfolio efficiency.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Identifying dominated portfolios</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The portfolios are:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Portfolio</th>\n<th>Expected return</th>\n<th>Standard deviation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>P</td>\n<td>8%</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Q</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>R</td>\n<td>12%</td>\n<td>18%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>S</td>\n<td>11%</td>\n<td>16%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Portfolio P and Portfolio Q have identical standard deviations of 10%.</p>\n<p>However:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R_Q)=10\\%&gt;E(R_P)=8\\%$$</div>\n<p>Therefore, Q provides a higher expected return for the same measured risk.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Q dominates P}}$$</div>\n<p>Portfolio P is consequently inefficient relative to the available alternatives.</p>\n<p>The remaining portfolios involve trade-offs between higher expected return and higher standard deviation.</p>\n<p>For example, Portfolio R has a higher expected return than S, but also higher volatility.</p>\n<p>Neither automatically dominates the other.</p>\n<p>Importantly, the fact that Q, R and S are not dominated by another option in this small table does not prove that each lies on the efficient frontier of the full investment opportunity set.</p>\n<p>Additional portfolio combinations might offer better trade-offs.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Efficient frontier and investor risk tolerance</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter defines the efficient frontier as the boundary of feasible portfolios offering the best attainable trade-offs between expected return and risk.</p>\n<p>A portfolio is inefficient if another available portfolio can provide a higher expected return without additional risk or the same expected return with less risk.</p>\n<p>The efficient frontier therefore identifies desirable combinations among the investment opportunities available under the assumed conditions.</p>\n<p>Investor risk tolerance determines which efficient combination may be suitable.</p>\n<p>A relatively risk-averse investor may prefer a lower-volatility portfolio because the investor places greater importance on limiting fluctuations.</p>\n<p>For example, among the given alternatives, Portfolio Q provides 10% expected return with 10% standard deviation.</p>\n<p>An investor with greater willingness and capacity to bear risk might prefer Portfolio S or R to pursue higher expected returns.</p>\n<p>However, choosing a lower-risk portfolio does not mean an investor should accept an inefficient portfolio.</p>\n<p>An investor who wants the lowest acceptable risk should still seek the best attainable return for that risk level.</p>\n<p>Evaluation: The frontier identifies efficient combinations, but it does not determine a single universally optimal portfolio for every investor.</p>\n<p>The investor's risk preferences and financial objectives remain important.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Reconciling traditional management and MPT</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter recommends combining the strengths of both approaches.</p>\n<p>A suitable investment process begins with determining how much risk the investor is willing to bear.</p>\n<p>The investor should then seek diversification across investment categories and industries while examining correlations between the securities.</p>\n<p>Portfolio beta can be used to assess systematic market sensitivity.</p>\n<p>Finally, the investor should compare alternative allocations and seek the highest expected return consistent with their acceptable risk.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the advisors should not rely solely on the number of securities held or on historical statistical optimization.</p>\n<p>They should combine economic judgment about individual securities with quantitative analysis of the portfolio as a whole.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Traditional portfolio management provides practical diversification principles, while MPT offers a more precise way to measure the risk-return consequences of combining investments. An effective portfolio strategy benefits from both.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><span>Portfolio beta and integrated investment decision (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>An investment fund manages two portfolios, V and W, based on Table 5.6 in the chapter.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Asset</th>\n<th>V weight</th>\n<th>V beta</th>\n<th>W weight</th>\n<th>W beta</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>0.10</td>\n<td>1.65</td>\n<td>0.10</td>\n<td>0.80</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>0.30</td>\n<td>1.00</td>\n<td>0.10</td>\n<td>1.00</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>0.20</td>\n<td>1.30</td>\n<td>0.20</td>\n<td>0.65</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>0.20</td>\n<td>1.10</td>\n<td>0.10</td>\n<td>0.75</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>0.20</td>\n<td>1.25</td>\n<td>0.50</td>\n<td>1.05</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td>1.00</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>1.00</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Assume the risk-free rate is 4% and the expected market return is 10%.</p>\n<p>(a) [6 marks] Calculate the beta of Portfolios V and W.</p>\n<p>(b) [6 marks] Calculate the CAPM-required return for both portfolios and explain their relative systematic risk.</p>\n<p>(c) [4 marks] Evaluate which portfolio may be more appropriate for a risk-averse investor, identifying what additional information would be required.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Propose how Portfolio W's market sensitivity could be reduced by changing allocation weights, and critically discuss the consequences.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate → Create</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 6 · Highest priority for numerical portfolio beta</em></p>\n<p>The chapter's Table 5.6 provides data for Austin Fund's Portfolios V and W.</p>\n<p><strong>(a) Calculating portfolio beta</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>Portfolio beta is the weighted average of the individual securities' betas.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\beta_p=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}w_i\\beta_i}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Portfolio V</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned} \\beta_V={}&amp;0.10(1.65)+0.30(1.00)\\\\ &amp;+0.20(1.30)+0.20(1.10)\\\\ &amp;+0.20(1.25) \\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=0.165+0.300+0.260+0.220+0.250$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\beta_V=1.195}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Portfolio W</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned} \\beta_W={}&amp;0.10(0.80)+0.10(1.00)\\\\ &amp;+0.20(0.65)+0.10(0.75)\\\\ &amp;+0.50(1.05) \\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=0.080+0.100+0.130+0.075+0.525$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\beta_W=0.910}$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Portfolio</th>\n<th>Beta</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>V</td>\n<td>1.195</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>W</td>\n<td>0.910</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Portfolio V has greater market sensitivity because its beta exceeds 1.</p>\n<p>Portfolio W has lower market sensitivity than the benchmark because its beta is below 1.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) CAPM-required returns</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>Given:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_f=4\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R_m)=10\\%$$</div>\n<p>The market risk premium is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$10\\%-4\\%=6\\%$$</div>\n<p>For Portfolio V:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_V=4\\%+1.195(6\\%)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_V=11.17\\%}$$</div>\n<p>For Portfolio W:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_W=4\\%+0.910(6\\%)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_W=9.46\\%}$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Portfolio V</th>\n<th>Portfolio W</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Portfolio beta</td>\n<td>1.195</td>\n<td>0.910</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CAPM-required return</td>\n<td>11.17%</td>\n<td>9.46%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Systematic market sensitivity</td>\n<td>Higher</td>\n<td>Lower</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Portfolio V requires greater expected compensation under CAPM because it bears more systematic risk.</p>\n<p>For illustration, if the market's return changes by -10 percentage points, the estimated market-linked changes are:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$V:1.195(-10\\%)=-11.95\\text{ percentage points}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$W:0.910(-10\\%)=-9.10\\text{ percentage points}$$</div>\n<p>These figures indicate market sensitivity, not guaranteed total realized portfolio returns.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Choosing a portfolio for a risk-averse investor</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>Portfolio W may be more appropriate for a risk-averse investor seeking lower systematic market exposure.</p>\n<p>Its beta of 0.910 indicates less sensitivity to the market than Portfolio V, whose beta is 1.195.</p>\n<p>However, this conclusion is only preliminary.</p>\n<p>A risk-averse investor requires adequate compensation for risk, but that does not mean the investor must always select the lowest-beta portfolio.</p>\n<p>The actual decision would also require estimates of each portfolio's expected return, total standard deviation, correlations among holdings and the investor's financial objectives.</p>\n<p>For example, Portfolio V might provide a sufficiently higher expected return to justify its additional market sensitivity.</p>\n<p>Conversely, Portfolio W may have substantial firm-specific volatility that is not apparent from beta alone.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: W has lower measured systematic market exposure, but additional information is required to determine which portfolio offers the more suitable overall risk-return trade-off.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Rebalancing Portfolio W</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>Portfolio beta can be changed by reallocating investment weights among securities with different betas.</p>\n<p>Consider a hypothetical adjustment to Portfolio W.</p>\n<p>Suppose the fund reduces Asset 5's weight from 50% to 40% and increases Asset 3's weight from 20% to 30%.</p>\n<p>Asset 5 has beta 1.05, while Asset 3 has beta 0.65.</p>\n<p>The revised portfolio weights become:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Asset</th>\n<th>Original weight</th>\n<th>Revised weight</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>20%</td>\n<td>30%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>50%</td>\n<td>40%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td>100%</td>\n<td>100%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The revised beta is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned} \\beta_{W,\\text{revised}}={}&amp;0.10(0.80)+0.10(1.00)\\\\ &amp;+0.30(0.65)+0.10(0.75)\\\\ &amp;+0.40(1.05) \\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=0.080+0.100+0.195+0.075+0.420$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\beta_{W,\\text{revised}}=0.870}$$</div>\n<p>The beta falls from:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$0.910\\rightarrow0.870$$</div>\n<p>This reduces the portfolio's estimated sensitivity to market movements.</p>\n<p>However, the adjustment could also affect expected return, total volatility and diversification.</p>\n<p>Reducing beta does not automatically improve portfolio efficiency or guarantee a reduction in every form of investment risk.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Rebalancing toward lower-beta securities can reduce market sensitivity, but the fund should evaluate the full portfolio risk-return consequences before implementing the adjustment.</p></div></details></div></details></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l5\" id=\"l5s-toc\"><summary><strong>Notes cover</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">8 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"1. How to structure a high-scoring Chapter 5 answer\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5s-s1\" title=\"1. How to structure a high-scoring Chapter 5 answer\"><span class=\"toc-no\">1</span>How to structure a high-scoring Chapter 5 answer</button></li><li data-full=\"2. Full mock subjective examination\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5s-s2\" title=\"2. Full mock subjective examination\"><span class=\"toc-no\">2</span>Full mock subjective examination</button></li><li data-full=\"3. Model answers and marking guidance\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5s-s3\" title=\"3. Model answers and marking guidance\"><span class=\"toc-no\">3</span>Model answers and marking guidance</button></li><li data-full=\"4. Additional short-answer questions for revision\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5s-s4\" title=\"4. Additional short-answer questions for revision\"><span class=\"toc-no\">4</span>Additional short-answer questions for revision</button></li><li data-full=\"5. Chapter 5 formula sheet\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5s-s5\" title=\"5. Chapter 5 formula sheet\"><span class=\"toc-no\">5</span>Chapter 5 formula sheet</button></li><li data-full=\"6. How to allocate marks in technical subjective questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5s-s6\" title=\"6. How to allocate marks in technical subjective questions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">6</span>Allocating marks in technical questions</button></li><li data-full=\"7. What I would prioritize for Chapter 5\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5s-s7\" title=\"7. What I would prioritize for Chapter 5\"><span class=\"toc-no\">7</span>What I would prioritize for Chapter 5</button></li><li data-full=\"8. Final higher-order examination exercise\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5s-s8\" title=\"8. Final higher-order examination exercise\"><span class=\"toc-no\">8</span>Final higher-order examination exercise</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"full-notes theme-l5\" open><summary><div><strong>Complete Chapter 5 subjective notes</strong><small>Every part of the subjective preparation, one dropdown per part.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>8 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><h2>Chapter 5 - Subjective Exam Preparation</h2>\n<p>Fundamentals of Investing · Modern Portfolio Concepts</p>\n<p>Chapter 5 introduces one of the most important ideas in investment management:</p>\n<p>An investment's risk cannot be fully evaluated in isolation. We must understand how it contributes to the risk and return of the entire portfolio.</p>\n<p>This chapter is particularly suitable for difficult subjective examinations because it combines statistical calculations, portfolio construction, diversification, CAPM and investment decision-making.</p>\n<p>For example, a basic question might ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Define correlation and explain diversification.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A higher-level question would ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Two assets have standard deviations of 20% and 10%. Explain how their portfolio risk changes as correlation falls from +1 to -1, and evaluate whether holding negatively correlated assets guarantees the elimination of risk.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The second question requires mathematical application, analysis of portfolio interactions and critical evaluation of assumptions.</p>\n<p>This preparation follows all six learning goals of your uploaded Chapter 5 slides, including portfolio objectives, correlation, international diversification, systematic and unsystematic risk, beta, CAPM, the Security Market Line, Modern Portfolio Theory, and portfolio management.</p><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5s-s1\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">1. How to structure a high-scoring Chapter 5 answer</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Chapter 5 subjective questions should be approached as portfolio decisions, not simply as statistical exercises.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Establish the investment principle:</strong> Define the appropriate concept: portfolio return, correlation, diversification, systematic risk, beta, CAPM or efficiency.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Apply the quantitative relationship:</strong> Calculate the relevant return, standard deviation, beta or CAPM-required return. Explain what each variable represents.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Analyse the economic mechanism:</strong> Explain how asset weights, co-movement, market exposure or return requirements affect the portfolio.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Evaluate and recommend:</strong> Compare alternatives, identify limitations and conclude which arrangement best satisfies the investor's risk tolerance and objectives.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Example: Weak answer versus strong answer</h4>\n<p>Question: Evaluate whether including a high-risk stock necessarily increases portfolio risk.</p>\n<p><strong>Weak answer:</strong></p>\n<p>Including a high-risk stock increases portfolio risk because its standard deviation is high. Investors should avoid risky stocks if they want a safe portfolio.</p>\n<p><strong>Stronger answer:</strong></p>\n<p>An asset's individual standard deviation measures the dispersion of its own returns, but its effect on portfolio risk also depends on its allocation weight and correlation with the existing holdings. Modern Portfolio Theory recognizes that assets with less than perfectly positive correlations can partially offset one another's fluctuations. Consequently, including a highly volatile stock may reduce total portfolio standard deviation if its diversification benefit is sufficient. For example, the chapter's IBM-Celgene portfolio shows that combining a relatively volatile stock with another investment can produce a portfolio less volatile than the less risky stock held alone. However, diversification benefits depend on return relationships and portfolio weights, which may change over time. Therefore, investors should evaluate the new stock's contribution to overall portfolio risk rather than rejecting it solely because its individual standard deviation is high.</p>\n<p>The stronger answer explains the mechanism, uses textbook evidence, identifies conditions and reaches a justified conclusion.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5s-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">2. Full mock subjective examination</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p><strong>Questions:</strong> 6 · <strong>Total marks:</strong> 120 · <strong>Suggested time:</strong> 120 min</p>\n<p>Each question carries 20 marks. The examination combines original investment scenarios with numerical data from Chapter 5's worked examples. Full model answers and illustrative marking guidance are provided below.</p>\n<h4>Question 1 - Portfolio objectives, return and standard deviation (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>An investor wishes to construct a portfolio using the following securities:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Security</th>\n<th>Portfolio weight</th>\n<th>Expected return</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Equity A</td>\n<td>50%</td>\n<td>12%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Equity B</td>\n<td>30%</td>\n<td>8%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bonds C</td>\n<td>20%</td>\n<td>5%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Separately, the investor is evaluating a two-asset portfolio with 50% invested in Security X and 50% in Security Y.</p>\n<p>Security X has a standard deviation of 20%, Security Y has a standard deviation of 10%, and their correlation coefficient is +0.50.</p>\n<p>(a) [4 marks] Explain the objectives of growth-oriented and income-oriented portfolios and define an efficient portfolio.</p>\n<p>(b) [4 marks] Calculate the expected return of the three-security portfolio.</p>\n<p>(c) [8 marks] Calculate the standard deviation of the equally weighted two-security portfolio. Recalculate it assuming correlation falls to -0.50.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Evaluate why portfolio risk cannot generally be determined by taking the weighted average of individual securities' standard deviations.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Understand → Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p>\n<h4>Question 2 - Correlation, diversification and international investing (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>An analyst examines three hypothetical securities, X, Y and Z, using the return patterns from the chapter's Table 5.3.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>X</th>\n<th>Y</th>\n<th>Z</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>8%</td>\n<td>24%</td>\n<td>8%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n<td>20%</td>\n<td>12%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>12%</td>\n<td>16%</td>\n<td>16%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>14%</td>\n<td>12%</td>\n<td>20%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>16%</td>\n<td>8%</td>\n<td>24%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Two portfolios are constructed:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$XY=\\frac23X+\\frac13Y$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$XZ=\\frac23X+\\frac13Z$$</div>\n<p>(a) [6 marks] Calculate the annual returns and average returns of both portfolios.</p>\n<p>(b) [4 marks] Compare their standard deviations and explain why their risk differs despite having similar average returns.</p>\n<p>(c) [4 marks] Explain how international diversification may improve portfolio performance, using the chapter's discussion of economic cycles and investment opportunities.</p>\n<p>(d) [6 marks] Critically evaluate the statement that perfectly negative correlation always eliminates investment risk, and explain the limitations of international diversification.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p>\n<h4>Question 3 - Systematic risk, unsystematic risk and beta (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>A portfolio manager holds shares in 40 companies across different industries.</p>\n<p>One company experiences a major employee strike, while another suffers a significant legal dispute.</p>\n<p>At the same time, an economy-wide financial crisis causes most stock prices to decline.</p>\n<p>Two securities within the portfolio have the following characteristics:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Security A</th>\n<th>Security B</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Beta</td>\n<td>1.50</td>\n<td>0.60</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total standard deviation</td>\n<td>30%</td>\n<td>40%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>(a) [6 marks] Differentiate systematic and unsystematic risk, applying each to the scenario.</p>\n<p>(b) [6 marks] Explain the meaning of beta and calculate the market-related return movements for Securities A and B if the market declines by 8%.</p>\n<p>(c) [4 marks] Evaluate which security has greater market sensitivity and which has greater total volatility.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Explain why diversification cannot eliminate all portfolio risk and why CAPM emphasizes systematic risk.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Understand → Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p>\n<h4>Question 4 - CAPM and the Security Market Line (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>An investment analyst assumes:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_f=6\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R_m)=10\\%$$</div>\n<p>The analyst evaluates three securities:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Security A</th>\n<th>Security B</th>\n<th>Security C</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Beta</td>\n<td>0.50</td>\n<td>1.00</td>\n<td>1.50</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Analyst's expected return</td>\n<td>9%</td>\n<td>9%</td>\n<td>14%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>(a) [4 marks] Explain the economic purpose of CAPM and derive its relationship between the risk-free rate, market risk premium and beta.</p>\n<p>(b) [6 marks] Calculate the CAPM-required return for each security.</p>\n<p>(c) [6 marks] Determine whether each security lies above or below the Security Market Line. Evaluate its attractiveness relative to the model.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Explain the intercept and slope of the SML and how changes in the risk-free rate or expected market risk premium affect the line.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p>\n<h4>Question 5 - Traditional portfolio management, MPT and the efficient frontier (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>A financial advisor manages a client's wealth using investments in several established companies from different industries.</p>\n<p>The advisor believes the portfolio is well diversified simply because it contains 30 securities.</p>\n<p>A second advisor uses Modern Portfolio Theory to estimate expected returns, standard deviations and correlations between securities.</p>\n<p>The advisors are considering these hypothetical portfolios:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Portfolio</th>\n<th>Expected return</th>\n<th>Standard deviation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>P</td>\n<td>8%</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Q</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>R</td>\n<td>12%</td>\n<td>18%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>S</td>\n<td>11%</td>\n<td>16%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>(a) [6 marks] Compare the traditional portfolio approach with Modern Portfolio Theory.</p>\n<p>(b) [4 marks] Identify any dominated portfolio among the alternatives and justify your answer.</p>\n<p>(c) [6 marks] Explain the efficient frontier and how an investor's risk tolerance influences portfolio selection.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Recommend how the advisors should combine traditional and modern portfolio management principles.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Analyse → Evaluate → Create</p>\n<h4>Question 6 - Portfolio beta and integrated investment decision (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>An investment fund manages two portfolios, V and W, based on Table 5.6 in the chapter.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Asset</th>\n<th>V weight</th>\n<th>V beta</th>\n<th>W weight</th>\n<th>W beta</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>0.10</td>\n<td>1.65</td>\n<td>0.10</td>\n<td>0.80</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>0.30</td>\n<td>1.00</td>\n<td>0.10</td>\n<td>1.00</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>0.20</td>\n<td>1.30</td>\n<td>0.20</td>\n<td>0.65</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>0.20</td>\n<td>1.10</td>\n<td>0.10</td>\n<td>0.75</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>0.20</td>\n<td>1.25</td>\n<td>0.50</td>\n<td>1.05</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td>1.00</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>1.00</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Assume the risk-free rate is 4% and the expected market return is 10%.</p>\n<p>(a) [6 marks] Calculate the beta of Portfolios V and W.</p>\n<p>(b) [6 marks] Calculate the CAPM-required return for both portfolios and explain their relative systematic risk.</p>\n<p>(c) [4 marks] Evaluate which portfolio may be more appropriate for a risk-averse investor, identifying what additional information would be required.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Propose how Portfolio W's market sensitivity could be reduced by changing allocation weights, and critically discuss the consequences.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate → Create</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5s-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">3. Model answers and marking guidance</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The following answers demonstrate how to combine calculations with economic reasoning. Mark allocations are illustrative and may differ from your lecturer's marking scheme.</p>\n<h4>Model Answer 1 - Portfolio objectives, return and standard deviation</h4>\n<p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 1 · High priority</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Portfolio objectives and efficiency</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>A portfolio is a collection of investments assembled to achieve one or more investment goals.</p>\n<p>The chapter distinguishes two major portfolio orientations.</p>\n<p>A growth-oriented portfolio emphasizes long-term capital appreciation. It may include securities whose main expected benefit is an increase in market value rather than regular current income.</p>\n<p>An income-oriented portfolio emphasizes cash distributions, including dividends and interest payments. It may be appropriate for investors requiring regular investment income.</p>\n<p>The ultimate objective is to construct an efficient portfolio.</p>\n<p>The chapter defines an efficient portfolio as one providing the highest expected return for a given level of risk.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Efficiency}=\\text{Best Attainable Risk-Return Trade-off}}$$</div>\n<p>An efficient portfolio is not necessarily the one with the highest absolute expected return. A higher-return portfolio may also involve substantially more risk.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Expected portfolio return</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter states that portfolio return is the weighted average of the constituent securities' expected returns.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{E(R_p)=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}w_iE(R_i)}$$</div>\n<p>Substituting:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R_p)=0.50(12\\%)+0.30(8\\%)+0.20(5\\%)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=6\\%+2.4\\%+1\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{E(R_p)=9.4\\%}$$</div>\n<p>The portfolio is expected to produce a 9.4% return based on the stated estimates.</p>\n<p>Its actual return may differ because expected returns are not guaranteed.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Calculating two-asset portfolio standard deviation</strong> [8 marks]</p>\n<p>Unlike expected return, portfolio standard deviation depends on correlations between asset returns.</p>\n<p>The two-asset portfolio variance formula is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ \\sigma_p^2= w_X^2\\sigma_X^2+ w_Y^2\\sigma_Y^2+ 2w_Xw_Y\\sigma_X\\sigma_Y\\rho_{XY} }$$</div>\n<p>The portfolio standard deviation is the square root of that variance.</p>\n<p>Given:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$w_X=w_Y=0.50$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sigma_X=0.20,\\qquad\\sigma_Y=0.10$$</div>\n<p>Situation 1: Correlation = +0.50</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned} \\sigma_p^2={}&amp;(0.5)^2(0.2)^2\\\\ &amp;+(0.5)^2(0.1)^2\\\\ &amp;+2(0.5)(0.5)(0.2)(0.1)(0.5) \\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=0.0100+0.0025+0.0050$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=0.0175$$</div>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sigma_p=\\sqrt{0.0175}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\sigma_p=13.23\\%}$$</div>\n<p>Situation 2: Correlation = -0.50</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned} \\sigma_p^2={}&amp;(0.5)^2(0.2)^2\\\\ &amp;+(0.5)^2(0.1)^2\\\\ &amp;+2(0.5)(0.5)(0.2)(0.1)(-0.5) \\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=0.0100+0.0025-0.0050$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=0.0075$$</div>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sigma_p=\\sqrt{0.0075}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\sigma_p=8.66\\%}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Effect of correlation on portfolio volatility</strong> (equal weights, individual volatilities of 20% and 10%):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\"> correlation ρ    portfolio σ\n     +0.5   ██████████████████████████          13.23%\n     -0.5   █████████████████                    8.66%\n            0%      4%      8%      12%     16%\n</code></pre>\n<p>Only the correlation changes. Portfolio weights and individual standard deviations remain constant.</p>\n<p>The portfolio's standard deviation declines from 13.23% to 8.66%.</p>\n<p>This reduction occurs because negative correlation allows movements in one investment to partially offset movements in the other.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Why portfolio risk is not a weighted average</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>A simple weighted average of individual standard deviations would produce:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$0.5(20\\%)+0.5(10\\%)=15\\%$$</div>\n<p>However, that result would apply to this long-only two-asset portfolio if the securities were perfectly positively correlated.</p>\n<p>The calculated portfolio standard deviations of 13.23% and 8.66% are lower because the correlation is less than +1.</p>\n<p>This demonstrates that the risk of combining investments depends not only on their individual volatility, but also on how their returns move together.</p>\n<p>The chapter's IBM-Celgene example provides similar evidence. It reports individual standard deviations of approximately 28.6% for IBM and 58.3% for Celgene, yet a portfolio weighted approximately 86% IBM and 14% Celgene has a standard deviation of approximately 25.3%.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: An investor should not reject a security solely because its individual volatility is high. The relevant question is how including that security affects the risk and expected return of the entire portfolio.</p>\n<h4>Model Answer 2 - Correlation, diversification and international investment</h4>\n<p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 2 · High priority</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Portfolio XY and XZ expected returns</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter's Table 5.3 uses the following asset combinations:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{XY}=\\frac23R_X+\\frac13R_Y$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{XZ}=\\frac23R_X+\\frac13R_Z$$</div>\n<p>For Year 1:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{XY}=\\frac23(8\\%)+\\frac13(24\\%)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=13.33\\%$$</div>\n<p>For the XZ portfolio:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{XZ}=\\frac23(8\\%)+\\frac13(8\\%)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=8\\%$$</div>\n<p>Repeating these calculations:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>XY return</th>\n<th>XZ return</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>13.33%</td>\n<td>8.00%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>13.33%</td>\n<td>10.67%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>13.33%</td>\n<td>13.33%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>13.33%</td>\n<td>16.00%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>13.33%</td>\n<td>18.67%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Average</td>\n<td>13.33%</td>\n<td>13.33%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Both portfolios have approximately equal average returns.</p>\n<p>However, their return patterns are substantially different.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Comparing risk</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>Portfolio XY produces approximately 13.33% every year in the table.</p>\n<p>Therefore, its standard deviation across the five illustrated returns is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\sigma_{XY}=0\\%}$$</div>\n<p>Portfolio XZ produces fluctuating returns.</p>\n<p>Using the sample standard deviation formula:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$s_{XZ}= \\sqrt{ \\frac{\\sum(R_{XZ,t}-\\bar R_{XZ})^2}{5-1} }$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{s_{XZ}\\approx4.22\\%}$$</div>\n<p>The chapter reports approximately 4.2% for portfolio XZ and 0% for portfolio XY.</p>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>X and Y move in opposite directions. As X's return increases, Y's return decreases.</p>\n<p>The selected portfolio weights allow their movements to cancel.</p>\n<p>By contrast, X and Z both increase through the five illustrated years, so their fluctuations reinforce one another instead of canceling.</p>\n<p>Therefore, XY dominates XZ under the chapter's measured return and risk dimensions.</p>\n<p>The important result is not that X or Y individually became safer. Their combination produced a more stable portfolio.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Advantages of international diversification</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter identifies international diversification as a way of expanding investment opportunities beyond the domestic securities market.</p>\n<p>First, foreign markets provide access to a broader range of securities and industries.</p>\n<p>Second, different economies may experience different business cycles. One market might be contracting while another is expanding.</p>\n<p>If the returns from these markets are not perfectly positively correlated, combining them may reduce overall portfolio volatility.</p>\n<p>Third, the slides suggest that research opportunities may exist in foreign markets that are less informationally efficient, although this is a potential opportunity rather than a guaranteed advantage.</p>\n<p>Thus, international diversification can potentially improve the portfolio's risk-return trade-off by introducing investments with different economic exposures.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Evaluating perfect negative correlation and international risks</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter explains that perfectly negative correlation can eliminate portfolio risk.</p>\n<p>However, this conclusion requires clarification.</p>\n<p>A correlation of -1 means two return series have an exact negative linear relationship. It does not mean any arbitrary combination of those assets must have zero standard deviation.</p>\n<p>For two assets to achieve complete variance cancellation, their portfolio weights must be appropriate.</p>\n<p>The relevant condition is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{w_X\\sigma_X=w_Y\\sigma_Y}$$</div>\n<p>when:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\rho_{XY}=-1$$</div>\n<p>For example, if one asset has a standard deviation of 20% and another has 10%, equal weighting does not fully offset their volatility.</p>\n<p>A one-third allocation to the first asset and two-thirds to the second can achieve full cancellation under the idealized assumptions.</p>\n<p>That mathematical qualification goes beyond the simplified wording of the slide but follows directly from the portfolio-variance relationship.</p>\n<p>Real investment markets present further limitations.</p>\n<p>Correlations are estimated from data and may change over time. Assets that were historically negatively correlated may move in the same direction during an economic crisis.</p>\n<p>International diversification also introduces currency exchange risk, potentially higher investment costs and practical difficulties associated with foreign investing.</p>\n<p>The chapter identifies these disadvantages alongside the benefits of international diversification.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Lower correlation can reduce portfolio risk, but the extent of risk reduction depends on the actual return relationships and allocation weights. International diversification is a useful risk-management strategy, not a guarantee against investment losses.</p>\n<h4>Model Answer 3 - Systematic risk, unsystematic risk and beta</h4>\n<p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 3 · High priority</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Components of portfolio risk</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter divides total portfolio risk into two broad components.</p>\n<p>Unsystematic risk, also called diversifiable risk, arises from firm-specific events.</p>\n<p>Examples include employee strikes, lawsuits and other developments affecting particular businesses.</p>\n<p>In this scenario, the employee strike and legal dispute represent unsystematic risks because their immediate causes are associated with individual companies.</p>\n<p>These risks may be reduced by holding a diversified portfolio rather than concentrating investment capital in only one or two firms.</p>\n<p>Systematic risk, also called nondiversifiable risk, arises from broader forces affecting many investments.</p>\n<p>Examples given in the chapter include war, inflation and political events.</p>\n<p>The economy-wide financial crisis represents systematic risk because it affects many securities simultaneously.</p>\n<p>The textbook's conceptual relationship is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Total Risk}=\\text{Systematic Risk}+\\text{Unsystematic Risk}}$$</div>\n<p>Diversification may reduce the unsystematic component substantially, but it does not eliminate the broad market exposure shared by many securities.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Beta and market-related returns</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter defines beta as a measure of nondiversifiable risk, showing how a security responds to market forces.</p>\n<p>The overall market has:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\beta_m=1}$$</div>\n<p>A security with beta greater than 1 is more sensitive to market movements than the benchmark.</p>\n<p>A security with beta between 0 and 1 has lower positive market sensitivity.</p>\n<p>The chapter illustrates the approximate market-related movement using:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Market-linked Return Movement}\\approx\\beta_i\\times\\text{Market Movement}$$</div>\n<p>In this scenario, the market declines by 8%.</p>\n<p><strong>Security A</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\beta_A=1.50$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$1.50(-8\\%)=-12\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Estimated market-linked movement}=-12\\%}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Security B</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\beta_B=0.60$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$0.60(-8\\%)=-4.8\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Estimated market-linked movement}=-4.8\\%}$$</div>\n<p>Security A has greater sensitivity to market fluctuations.</p>\n<p>However, these are simplified market-related estimates, consistent with the slide's interpretation of beta. They do not guarantee either security's actual return, which can also be influenced by firm-specific factors.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Beta versus standard deviation</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The securities have the following characteristics:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Measure</th>\n<th>Security A</th>\n<th>Security B</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Beta</td>\n<td>1.50</td>\n<td>0.60</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Standard deviation</td>\n<td>30%</td>\n<td>40%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Security A has the higher beta, so it is more sensitive to market movements.</p>\n<p>Security B has the higher standard deviation, so its total returns are more volatile based on the given measure.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Higher Beta}=\\text{Greater Market Sensitivity}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Higher SD}=\\text{Greater Total Return Volatility}}$$</div>\n<p>It would be incorrect to declare that Security A is more volatile in every sense merely because its beta is larger.</p>\n<p>Likewise, Security B should not automatically be considered more exposed to systematic market movements just because its standard deviation is higher.</p>\n<p>This distinction is important because beta and standard deviation measure different dimensions of investment risk.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Why systematic risk matters for CAPM</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>As more appropriately diversified securities are added to a portfolio, firm-specific fluctuations can increasingly offset one another.</p>\n<p>However, common market forces may affect many securities simultaneously.</p>\n<p>The chapter's Figure 5.8 illustrates portfolio risk declining as diversification increases before approaching a residual systematic-risk level.</p>\n<p>CAPM focuses on systematic risk because the model assumes investors can diversify away firm-specific risk.</p>\n<p>Consequently, under its framework, investors are compensated for bearing nondiversifiable market exposure rather than every source of individual asset volatility.</p>\n<p>This explains why a security with a high standard deviation does not automatically require a higher CAPM return than a security with lower standard deviation.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Beta helps measure market-related risk, while standard deviation measures total volatility. A well-diversified investor must distinguish between these risk measures rather than treating all investment uncertainty as equivalent.</p>\n<h4>Model Answer 4 - CAPM and the Security Market Line</h4>\n<p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 4 · Highest priority</em></p>\n<p>This question is particularly important because CAPM combines the chapter's treatment of systematic risk with the idea of a required investment return.</p>\n<p><strong>(a) CAPM and its economic meaning</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM) links an investment's required return to its exposure to systematic market risk.</p>\n<p>The model uses three inputs: the risk-free rate, the expected market return and the security's beta.</p>\n<p>The equation is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ R_i=R_f+\\beta_i[E(R_m)-R_f] }$$</div>\n<p>where:</p>\n<p>$R_i$ = CAPM-required return.</p>\n<p>$R_f$ = risk-free rate.</p>\n<p>$\\beta_i$ = security's systematic market-risk sensitivity.</p>\n<p>$E(R_m)$ = expected market return.</p>\n<p>The difference:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R_m)-R_f$$</div>\n<p>is the market risk premium.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ \\text{Required Return} = \\text{Risk-Free Rate} + \\text{Beta-Adjusted Market Risk Premium} }$$</div>\n<p>The risk-free rate establishes the baseline required return.</p>\n<p>The market risk premium compensates investors for bearing market-related risk, while beta determines how much of that compensation is required for a particular security.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Calculating CAPM-required returns</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>Given:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_f=6\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R_m)=10\\%$$</div>\n<p>The market risk premium is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$10\\%-6\\%=\\boxed{4\\%}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Security A</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_A=6\\%+0.5(4\\%)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_A=8\\%}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Security B</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_B=6\\%+1.0(4\\%)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_B=10\\%}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Security C</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_C=6\\%+1.5(4\\%)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_C=12\\%}$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Security</th>\n<th>Beta</th>\n<th>CAPM-required return</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>0.50</td>\n<td>8%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>1.00</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>1.50</td>\n<td>12%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>As beta increases, the required return increases because investors require more compensation for systematic market exposure.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Evaluating securities relative to the SML</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The Security Market Line (SML) represents the CAPM relationship graphically.</p>\n<p>The vertical axis shows required return, while the horizontal axis shows beta.</p>\n<p>For this example:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_i=6\\%+4\\%\\beta_i}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Security Market Line</strong> (CAPM with a 6% risk-free rate and a 4% market risk premium):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\"> required\n return\n  14% ┤                         ·\n      │                      ·\n  12% ┤                   ● C: β = 1.5 → 12%\n      │                ·\n  10% ┤             ● B: β = 1.0 → 10%\n      │          ·\n   8% ┤       ● A: β = 0.5 → 8%\n      │    ·\n   6% ┤ ● Rf (β = 0)\n      └────────────────────────────► beta (β)\n             0.5    1    1.5    2\n\n  A security ABOVE the line offers more than CAPM requires (positive alpha);\n  a security BELOW the line offers less (negative alpha).\n</code></pre>\n<p>Compare the required returns with the analyst's expected returns.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Security</th>\n<th>Expected return</th>\n<th>Required return</th>\n<th>Expected alpha</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>9%</td>\n<td>8%</td>\n<td>+1 percentage point</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>9%</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n<td>-1 percentage point</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>14%</td>\n<td>12%</td>\n<td>+2 percentage points</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Security A</strong></p>\n<p>The estimated expected return exceeds the CAPM-required return by one percentage point.</p>\n<p>The security therefore lies above the SML.</p>\n<p>Under the model and forecast assumptions, it provides more expected return than required for its beta.</p>\n<p><strong>Security B</strong></p>\n<p>The expected return is 9%, compared with a required return of 10%.</p>\n<p>Security B lies below the SML.</p>\n<p>The analyst's forecast does not provide sufficient expected compensation for its systematic risk under CAPM.</p>\n<p><strong>Security C</strong></p>\n<p>The expected return is 14%, compared with a required return of 12%.</p>\n<p>Security C lies above the SML by two percentage points.</p>\n<p>It has the largest positive expected alpha among the three securities.</p>\n<p>However, it also has the highest beta.</p>\n<p>Therefore, investors should not confuse the highest expected alpha with the lowest risk.</p>\n<p>Evaluation: Securities A and C appear attractive relative to CAPM, while Security B appears unattractive. But these conclusions depend on the accuracy of beta, expected returns and the model's assumptions.</p>\n<p>Positive expected alpha is not a guaranteed realized return.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Intercept and slope of the SML</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The equation is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_i=R_f+\\beta_i[E(R_m)-R_f]$$</div>\n<p>The vertical intercept is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_f=6\\%}$$</div>\n<p>This is the required return when beta equals zero.</p>\n<p>The slope is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{E(R_m)-R_f=4\\%}$$</div>\n<p>It represents the increase in required return for each additional unit of beta.</p>\n<p>If the risk-free rate increases while the market risk premium is held constant, the SML shifts upward in parallel.</p>\n<p>If the market risk premium increases while the risk-free rate is held constant, the SML becomes steeper.</p>\n<p>These effects differ because the intercept and slope represent different economic components.</p>\n<p>The chapter's Figure 5.6 uses a 6% risk-free rate and shows required returns increasing along the SML as beta increases.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: CAPM provides a benchmark for evaluating expected compensation for systematic risk. Investors should use it to compare expected returns with required returns rather than merely selecting securities with the highest beta or highest forecast return.</p>\n<p><strong>Examiner's key distinction:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Expected Return}&gt;\\text{CAPM Return}}$$</div>\n<p>This suggests a security lies above the SML under the model's assumptions.</p>\n<p>It does not prove that the investment will outperform in reality.</p>\n<h4>Model Answer 5 - Traditional management, MPT and the efficient frontier</h4>\n<p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 5 · High priority</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Traditional versus modern portfolio management</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter distinguishes two main approaches to constructing investment portfolios.</p>\n<p>The traditional approach emphasizes balancing investments across a variety of securities and industries.</p>\n<p>It also tends to favor established, recognizable companies because investors may perceive them as safer, more liquid and easier to understand.</p>\n<p>In contrast, Modern Portfolio Theory (MPT) emphasizes statistical relationships among securities.</p>\n<p>It focuses on expected returns, standard deviations and correlations.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Dimension</th>\n<th>Traditional approach</th>\n<th>Modern Portfolio Theory</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Primary method</td>\n<td>Diversification across investments and industries</td>\n<td>Quantitative portfolio analysis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main consideration</td>\n<td>Investment characteristics and perceived suitability</td>\n<td>Expected returns, volatility and correlation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Typical emphasis</td>\n<td>Established securities and broad diversification</td>\n<td>Efficient risk-return combinations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main limitation</td>\n<td>Number and variety of holdings may not reveal their actual co-movement</td>\n<td>Results depend on the reliability of return and correlation estimates</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The central difference is that traditional management emphasizes diversification by security and industry, whereas MPT evaluates diversification through the relationships between securities' returns.</p>\n<p>A portfolio containing 30 stocks may appear diversified.</p>\n<p>However, if most securities respond similarly to market conditions, they may provide limited diversification benefits.</p>\n<p>Thus, the number of securities held is not enough to establish portfolio efficiency.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Identifying dominated portfolios</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The portfolios are:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Portfolio</th>\n<th>Expected return</th>\n<th>Standard deviation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>P</td>\n<td>8%</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Q</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>R</td>\n<td>12%</td>\n<td>18%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>S</td>\n<td>11%</td>\n<td>16%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Portfolio P and Portfolio Q have identical standard deviations of 10%.</p>\n<p>However:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R_Q)=10\\%&gt;E(R_P)=8\\%$$</div>\n<p>Therefore, Q provides a higher expected return for the same measured risk.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Q dominates P}}$$</div>\n<p>Portfolio P is consequently inefficient relative to the available alternatives.</p>\n<p>The remaining portfolios involve trade-offs between higher expected return and higher standard deviation.</p>\n<p>For example, Portfolio R has a higher expected return than S, but also higher volatility.</p>\n<p>Neither automatically dominates the other.</p>\n<p>Importantly, the fact that Q, R and S are not dominated by another option in this small table does not prove that each lies on the efficient frontier of the full investment opportunity set.</p>\n<p>Additional portfolio combinations might offer better trade-offs.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Efficient frontier and investor risk tolerance</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter defines the efficient frontier as the boundary of feasible portfolios offering the best attainable trade-offs between expected return and risk.</p>\n<p>A portfolio is inefficient if another available portfolio can provide a higher expected return without additional risk or the same expected return with less risk.</p>\n<p>The efficient frontier therefore identifies desirable combinations among the investment opportunities available under the assumed conditions.</p>\n<p>Investor risk tolerance determines which efficient combination may be suitable.</p>\n<p>A relatively risk-averse investor may prefer a lower-volatility portfolio because the investor places greater importance on limiting fluctuations.</p>\n<p>For example, among the given alternatives, Portfolio Q provides 10% expected return with 10% standard deviation.</p>\n<p>An investor with greater willingness and capacity to bear risk might prefer Portfolio S or R to pursue higher expected returns.</p>\n<p>However, choosing a lower-risk portfolio does not mean an investor should accept an inefficient portfolio.</p>\n<p>An investor who wants the lowest acceptable risk should still seek the best attainable return for that risk level.</p>\n<p>Evaluation: The frontier identifies efficient combinations, but it does not determine a single universally optimal portfolio for every investor.</p>\n<p>The investor's risk preferences and financial objectives remain important.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Reconciling traditional management and MPT</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter recommends combining the strengths of both approaches.</p>\n<p>A suitable investment process begins with determining how much risk the investor is willing to bear.</p>\n<p>The investor should then seek diversification across investment categories and industries while examining correlations between the securities.</p>\n<p>Portfolio beta can be used to assess systematic market sensitivity.</p>\n<p>Finally, the investor should compare alternative allocations and seek the highest expected return consistent with their acceptable risk.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the advisors should not rely solely on the number of securities held or on historical statistical optimization.</p>\n<p>They should combine economic judgment about individual securities with quantitative analysis of the portfolio as a whole.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Traditional portfolio management provides practical diversification principles, while MPT offers a more precise way to measure the risk-return consequences of combining investments. An effective portfolio strategy benefits from both.</p>\n<h4>Model Answer 6 - Portfolio beta and investment decisions</h4>\n<p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 6 · Highest priority for numerical portfolio beta</em></p>\n<p>The chapter's Table 5.6 provides data for Austin Fund's Portfolios V and W.</p>\n<p><strong>(a) Calculating portfolio beta</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>Portfolio beta is the weighted average of the individual securities' betas.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\beta_p=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}w_i\\beta_i}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Portfolio V</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned} \\beta_V={}&amp;0.10(1.65)+0.30(1.00)\\\\ &amp;+0.20(1.30)+0.20(1.10)\\\\ &amp;+0.20(1.25) \\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=0.165+0.300+0.260+0.220+0.250$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\beta_V=1.195}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Portfolio W</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned} \\beta_W={}&amp;0.10(0.80)+0.10(1.00)\\\\ &amp;+0.20(0.65)+0.10(0.75)\\\\ &amp;+0.50(1.05) \\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=0.080+0.100+0.130+0.075+0.525$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\beta_W=0.910}$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Portfolio</th>\n<th>Beta</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>V</td>\n<td>1.195</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>W</td>\n<td>0.910</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Portfolio V has greater market sensitivity because its beta exceeds 1.</p>\n<p>Portfolio W has lower market sensitivity than the benchmark because its beta is below 1.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) CAPM-required returns</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>Given:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_f=4\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R_m)=10\\%$$</div>\n<p>The market risk premium is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$10\\%-4\\%=6\\%$$</div>\n<p>For Portfolio V:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_V=4\\%+1.195(6\\%)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_V=11.17\\%}$$</div>\n<p>For Portfolio W:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_W=4\\%+0.910(6\\%)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_W=9.46\\%}$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Portfolio V</th>\n<th>Portfolio W</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Portfolio beta</td>\n<td>1.195</td>\n<td>0.910</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CAPM-required return</td>\n<td>11.17%</td>\n<td>9.46%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Systematic market sensitivity</td>\n<td>Higher</td>\n<td>Lower</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Portfolio V requires greater expected compensation under CAPM because it bears more systematic risk.</p>\n<p>For illustration, if the market's return changes by -10 percentage points, the estimated market-linked changes are:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$V:1.195(-10\\%)=-11.95\\text{ percentage points}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$W:0.910(-10\\%)=-9.10\\text{ percentage points}$$</div>\n<p>These figures indicate market sensitivity, not guaranteed total realized portfolio returns.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Choosing a portfolio for a risk-averse investor</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>Portfolio W may be more appropriate for a risk-averse investor seeking lower systematic market exposure.</p>\n<p>Its beta of 0.910 indicates less sensitivity to the market than Portfolio V, whose beta is 1.195.</p>\n<p>However, this conclusion is only preliminary.</p>\n<p>A risk-averse investor requires adequate compensation for risk, but that does not mean the investor must always select the lowest-beta portfolio.</p>\n<p>The actual decision would also require estimates of each portfolio's expected return, total standard deviation, correlations among holdings and the investor's financial objectives.</p>\n<p>For example, Portfolio V might provide a sufficiently higher expected return to justify its additional market sensitivity.</p>\n<p>Conversely, Portfolio W may have substantial firm-specific volatility that is not apparent from beta alone.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: W has lower measured systematic market exposure, but additional information is required to determine which portfolio offers the more suitable overall risk-return trade-off.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Rebalancing Portfolio W</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>Portfolio beta can be changed by reallocating investment weights among securities with different betas.</p>\n<p>Consider a hypothetical adjustment to Portfolio W.</p>\n<p>Suppose the fund reduces Asset 5's weight from 50% to 40% and increases Asset 3's weight from 20% to 30%.</p>\n<p>Asset 5 has beta 1.05, while Asset 3 has beta 0.65.</p>\n<p>The revised portfolio weights become:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Asset</th>\n<th>Original weight</th>\n<th>Revised weight</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>20%</td>\n<td>30%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>50%</td>\n<td>40%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td>100%</td>\n<td>100%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The revised beta is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{aligned} \\beta_{W,\\text{revised}}={}&amp;0.10(0.80)+0.10(1.00)\\\\ &amp;+0.30(0.65)+0.10(0.75)\\\\ &amp;+0.40(1.05) \\end{aligned}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=0.080+0.100+0.195+0.075+0.420$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\beta_{W,\\text{revised}}=0.870}$$</div>\n<p>The beta falls from:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$0.910\\rightarrow0.870$$</div>\n<p>This reduces the portfolio's estimated sensitivity to market movements.</p>\n<p>However, the adjustment could also affect expected return, total volatility and diversification.</p>\n<p>Reducing beta does not automatically improve portfolio efficiency or guarantee a reduction in every form of investment risk.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Rebalancing toward lower-beta securities can reduce market sensitivity, but the fund should evaluate the full portfolio risk-return consequences before implementing the adjustment.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5s-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">4. Additional short-answer questions for revision</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>These questions are suitable for 5-10 marks each. Try to explain the financial mechanism before revealing the model answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Q1. Explain why adding a highly volatile security can reduce the total risk of an existing portfolio.</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Portfolio standard deviation depends on individual asset weights, their standard deviations and the correlations among their returns. If a highly volatile security has sufficiently low or negative covariance with existing holdings, its return movements can offset fluctuations elsewhere in the portfolio. Therefore, the combined portfolio may have lower risk than the original portfolio, despite the new security's high individual volatility. This is illustrated by the chapter's IBM-Celgene example.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q2. Differentiate positive correlation, zero correlation and negative correlation in portfolio diversification.</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Positive correlation indicates that asset returns tend to move in the same direction. Zero correlation indicates no linear co-movement. Negative correlation indicates that returns tend to move in opposite directions. Holding weights and individual volatilities constant, lower correlation generally reduces portfolio standard deviation. Perfect positive correlation provides no diversification reduction relative to the weighted average of standard deviations, while perfect negative correlation can eliminate variance when appropriate weights are selected.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q3. Why does diversification have diminishing benefits as additional securities are added?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Diversification reduces exposure to firm-specific unsystematic risk. Initially, adding securities with different return behavior can significantly lower portfolio variability. However, as the portfolio becomes better diversified, much of the firm-specific risk has already been reduced. Broader market factors continue to affect many securities simultaneously, leaving a systematic-risk component that ordinary diversification cannot eliminate. This relationship is illustrated in Figure 5.8.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q4. Why is beta a more relevant pricing measure than standard deviation under CAPM?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Beta measures sensitivity to systematic market risk, while standard deviation measures total return variability. CAPM assumes investors can diversify away firm-specific risk. Therefore, its required-return relationship compensates investors for systematic risk rather than all standalone volatility. A security with higher total standard deviation can have lower beta if much of its variability is not associated with market movements.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q5. Explain how the SML changes when the market risk premium increases.</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The SML follows Ri = Rf + βi[E(Rm) - Rf]. Its slope is the market risk premium. If the market risk premium increases while the risk-free rate stays constant, the line becomes steeper. Securities with higher beta experience larger increases in required return. By contrast, changing the risk-free rate while holding the market risk premium constant produces a parallel shift in the SML.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q6. Why does a security above the SML appear attractive under CAPM?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A security above the SML offers an estimated expected return greater than the CAPM-required return for its beta. This produces positive expected alpha relative to the model. Investors may interpret this as favorable expected compensation or potential undervaluation relative to the model. However, the conclusion depends on the accuracy of beta, expected returns and CAPM assumptions. It does not guarantee future outperformance.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q7. Explain the difference between the efficient frontier and the Security Market Line.</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The efficient frontier represents attainable portfolio combinations offering the best expected-return/standard-deviation trade-offs. Its horizontal axis measures portfolio standard deviation. The SML represents CAPM-required return as a function of systematic risk measured by beta. Therefore, the frontier supports portfolio construction, while the SML provides a risk-based required-return benchmark.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q8. Evaluate whether a portfolio containing 50 different stocks must be better diversified than a portfolio containing 15 stocks.</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> No. The number of securities alone does not establish diversification effectiveness. A 50-stock portfolio concentrated in one industry may contain substantial common exposure, whereas 15 securities with different economic and return characteristics may offset one another more effectively. Proper evaluation requires examining portfolio weights, correlations, industry exposures and market sensitivity. More holdings do not necessarily mean a more efficient portfolio.</p>\n</blockquote></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5s-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">5. Chapter 5 formula sheet</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l5_frontier\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Only the upper-left edge of the feasible set is efficient; the utility curve picks the investor&#x27;s point on it.</figcaption></figure></div><p>These are the core formulas and mathematical applications you should understand.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Formula</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Portfolio expected return</td>\n<td>$E(R_p)=\\sum w_iE(R_i)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Two-asset portfolio variance</td>\n<td>$\\sigma_p^2=w_A^2\\sigma_A^2+w_B^2\\sigma_B^2+2w_Aw_B\\sigma_A\\sigma_B\\rho_{AB}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Portfolio standard deviation</td>\n<td>$\\sigma_p=\\sqrt{\\sigma_p^2}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Correlation range</td>\n<td>$-1\\le\\rho_{AB}\\le1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Individual beta - mathematical definition</td>\n<td>$\\beta_i=\\frac{\\operatorname{Cov}(R_i,R_m)}{\\operatorname{Var}(R_m)}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Portfolio beta</td>\n<td>$\\beta_p=\\sum w_i\\beta_i$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Market risk premium</td>\n<td>$E(R_m)-R_f$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CAPM-required return</td>\n<td>$R_i=R_f+\\beta_i[E(R_m)-R_f]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Expected CAPM alpha</td>\n<td>$\\alpha_i=E(R_i)-R_{i,\\text{CAPM}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The chapter directly teaches portfolio return, the role of correlations in portfolio standard deviation, beta, portfolio beta and CAPM. The explicit covariance form of the two-asset variance equation, individual-beta formula and expected-alpha calculation are mathematical formulations or extensions of those concepts.</p>\n<h4>Selecting the right formula</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Exam scenario</th>\n<th>Appropriate method</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Expected return for a portfolio of three stocks</td>\n<td>Weighted-average portfolio return</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Portfolio volatility given two standard deviations and correlation</td>\n<td>Two-asset portfolio standard deviation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evaluating how asset co-movement affects diversification</td>\n<td>Correlation and covariance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Portfolio beta given asset weights and betas</td>\n<td>Weighted-average portfolio beta</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Finding the required return for beta 1.4</td>\n<td>CAPM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Comparing expected return with CAPM-required return</td>\n<td>SML position / expected alpha</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Choosing between portfolios with different SD and returns</td>\n<td>Efficient frontier / portfolio dominance</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A common mistake is applying the weighted-average formula to portfolio standard deviation simply because it works for expected return and beta.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5s-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">6. How to allocate marks in technical subjective questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>For a typical 20-mark problem requiring calculations and evaluation, this is a useful practice guide:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Answer component</th>\n<th>Illustrative marks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Correct concepts and definitions</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mathematical formula and accurate calculation</td>\n<td>6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Economic interpretation and analysis</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evaluation, assumptions and justified conclusion</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td>20</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A calculation may be perfectly correct without providing a complete answer.</p>\n<p>For example, calculating:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\beta_p=1.195$$</div>\n<p>does not itself demonstrate that you understand the investment implications.</p>\n<p>You should explain that the portfolio has approximately 1.195 times the benchmark's market-return sensitivity under the beta model.</p>\n<p>You should also distinguish that market exposure from total portfolio volatility, which cannot be calculated from beta alone.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5s-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">7. What I would prioritize for Chapter 5</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Highest priority - Correlation and portfolio diversification</p>\n<p>Understand the mathematics of portfolio variance, the implications of positive and negative correlation, and why a risky individual security may improve a portfolio.</p>\n<p>Highest priority - CAPM and portfolio beta</p>\n<p>Practise calculating beta, portfolio beta and required returns. Be able to explain market sensitivity and compensation for systematic risk.</p>\n<p>High priority - Security Market Line</p>\n<p>Interpret the intercept and slope, identify securities above or below the SML, and evaluate expected returns relative to required returns.</p>\n<p>High priority - Systematic versus unsystematic risk</p>\n<p>Differentiate firm-specific risks from broad market risks and explain why ordinary diversification cannot eliminate the systematic component.</p>\n<p>High priority - Efficient frontier and MPT</p>\n<p>Identify dominated portfolios, explain what makes a portfolio efficient, and show how investor risk tolerance influences portfolio selection.</p>\n<p>Important - International diversification and portfolio management</p>\n<p>Evaluate both the benefits and risks of foreign securities, then reconcile traditional management with quantitative portfolio analysis.</p>\n<p>These are priorities based on technical depth and likely opportunities for higher-order questions, rather than verified lecturer-specific weightings.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5s-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">8. Final higher-order examination exercise</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Evaluate the following statement:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"An investor should construct a portfolio using only the stocks with the highest expected returns. Adding lower-return securities reduces portfolio performance, while diversification merely sacrifices potential profits.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A high-scoring response should challenge the statement using several Chapter 5 principles.</p>\n<p>First, expected portfolio return is a weighted average of individual expected returns, but expected return alone does not describe portfolio suitability.</p>\n<p>Second, portfolio volatility depends on individual standard deviations, portfolio weights and correlations.</p>\n<p>Third, a security with a relatively low individual expected return may provide a diversification benefit if it has sufficiently low covariance with existing holdings.</p>\n<p>Fourth, efficient portfolio construction seeks the best available trade-off between expected return and risk, rather than maximizing expected return without considering volatility.</p>\n<p>Finally, the investor must assess systematic risk through beta and compare expected compensation with the CAPM-required return where that model is being applied.</p>\n<p>A well-constructed portfolio should therefore be evaluated as an integrated combination of securities rather than a collection of individually attractive assets.</p>\n<p>The central Chapter 5 relationship is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ \\text{Portfolio Risk} \\neq \\text{Weighted Average of Individual Risks} }$$</div>\n<p>And the broader investment decision is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ \\text{Portfolio Selection} = f(\\text{Expected Returns, Weights, Volatility, Correlation, Beta, Risk Tolerance}) }$$</div>\n<p>The deepest lesson of Chapter 5 is that superior portfolio management is not merely about finding good investments. It is about combining investments whose characteristics interact to produce the most suitable overall risk-return outcome.</p>\n<p>That is the level of understanding you should demonstrate consistently in your subjective examination.</p>\n<p>Source note: Prepared using your uploaded Chapter 5 - Modern Portfolio Concepts (Pearson, 2014). The IBM-Celgene, X/Y/Z and Austin Fund examples follow the textbook. Other scenarios, evaluation criteria and marking allocations are original practice material. CAPM, beta and portfolio-variance calculations use the stated model assumptions and should not be interpreted as guaranteed real-world investment outcomes.</p></div></details></div></details>");
